<GuideLayout 
  title="Screen Printing vs. Heat Transfer: Choosing the Right Method" 
  type="LOGO PRINTING"
  description="Compare durability, cost, and visual finish. Choose the best printing technique for your activewear logos and branding."
>
  <h2>Branding Your Collection</h2>
  <p>The method you choose to apply your logo can affect everything from the garment's stretch to its overall cost. Here is how to choose the right one for your brand.</p>
  
  <h3>Screen Printing (Traditional & Durable)</h3>
  <p>Ink is pushed through a stencil onto the fabric. 
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pros:</strong> High durability, great for large batches, and vibrant colors.</li>
      <li><strong>Cons:</strong> High setup costs per color, not ideal for gradients, and can feel 'stiff' on high-stretch fabrics.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <h3>Heat Transfer (Flexible & Precise)</h3>
  <p>A logo is printed onto a carrier sheet and then pressed onto the fabric with heat.
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Low setup costs, handles complex details/gradients well, and maintains the fabric's stretch (if using specialized stretch transfers).</li>
      <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Slightly less durable than screen printing over dozens of washes if not applied correctly.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <h3>Silicone Printing (The Premium Choice)</h3>
  <p>A specialized silicone ink is used to create a 3D, high-grip effect. This is the gold standard for premium activewear, providing a luxurious feel and extreme durability.</p>

  <h3>Our Recommendation</h3>
  <p>For small startup batches (100-200 pcs) with multi-color logos, <strong>Heat Transfer</strong> is usually the best value. For large, single-color branding on gym wear, <strong>Screen Printing</strong> is the most economical.</p>
</GuideLayout>
